    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>’s viceroyalty for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a>, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a>, which at first covered nearly all of Spanish South America.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">Silver</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a>, mined with forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mita">mita</a> labour, financed the Spanish crown and flowed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> cut its territory in the eighteenth century, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> in 1824 ended Spanish rule.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first book printed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a> was a catechism in Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aymara">Aymara</a>, published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a> in 1584.</p></aside>
    </section>

