    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#francisco-pizarro"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-pizarro.jpg" alt="Francisco Pizarro" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Francisco_Pizarro.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Amable-Paul Coutan, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> grew out of the conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inca-empire">Inca Empire</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-pizarro">Francisco Pizarro</a> and his companions, who captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sapa-inca">Sapa Inca</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#atahualpa">Atahualpa</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cajamarca">Cajamarca</a> in 1532 and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cusco">Cuzco</a> in 1533. The Crown first divided the conquered lands into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#governorate">governorates</a> of New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>, granted to Pizarro, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#governorate-of-new-toledo">New Toledo</a>, granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#diego-de-almagro">Diego de Almagro</a>, but rivalry between the two conquerors led to civil war, the execution of Almagro in 1538 and the assassination of Pizarro in 1541 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a>, the city he had founded in 1535. To impose royal authority, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> issued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-laws">New Laws of 1542</a>, which created the viceroyalty with its seat at Lima and restricted the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#encomienda">encomiendas</a></em>, the grants of indigenous tribute and labour held by the conquerors. The first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#blasco-nunez-vela">Blasco Núñez Vela</a>, arrived in 1544, but his attempt to enforce the New Laws provoked a revolt of encomenderos under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gonzalo-pizarro">Gonzalo Pizarro</a>, who defeated and killed him at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-anaquito">Battle of Añaquito</a> in 1546. The cleric <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pedro-de-la-gasca">Pedro de la Gasca</a>, sent with broad powers and a promise to soften the laws, won over most of the rebels and defeated Gonzalo Pizarro at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-jaquijahuana">Jaquijahuana</a> in 1548, after which Pizarro was executed. A further rising under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-hernandez-giron">Francisco Hernández Girón</a> in 1553–1554 was suppressed, and royal government was then secure.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#huancavelica"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/huancavelica.jpg" alt="Huancavelica" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#huancavelica">Huancavelica</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:391239379_81ed2d3789_o.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jorge Gobbi from Buenos Aires, Argentina, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Colonial Peruvian society was formally divided into a <em>república de españoles</em> and a <em>república de indios</em>, each with its own laws, courts and obligations. Indigenous communities paid tribute and were governed through their hereditary lords, the <em>kurakas</em>, who mediated between villagers and Spanish officials, while the Andean population fell steeply in the sixteenth century through epidemics, war and forced labour. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cerro-rico">Cerro Rico of Potosí</a>, discovered in 1545, became the largest source of silver in the world, and after mercury amalgamation was introduced in the 1570s its output soared, supplied with mercury from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#huancavelica">Huancavelica</a>, discovered in 1563. Through the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mita">mita</a></em>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-alvarez-de-toledo">Francisco de Toledo</a> assigned some 13,000 indigenous men a year from sixteen highland provinces to work at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a>, a burden that drove many to abandon their communities. Silver travelled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#armada-del-mar-del-sur">Armada del Mar del Sur</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#callao">Callao</a> to Panama and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seville">Seville</a>, and by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#acapulco">Acapulco</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila-galleon">Manila galleons</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a>. Coastal estates grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and vines with the labour of enslaved Africans, and textile workshops, the <em>obrajes</em>, supplied cloth. The Catholic Church organised parishes, missions and confraternities, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a> produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rose-of-lima">Rose of Lima</a>, canonised in 1671 as the first saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#martin-de-porres">Martin de Porres</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rancagua"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rancagua.jpg" alt="Battle of Rancagua" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rancagua">Battle of Rancagua</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Carga_de_O%C2%B4Higgins_en_Rancagua.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pedro Subercaseaux, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For much of its history the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> relied less on a standing army than on the loyalty of its settlers and the distance that shielded it from European rivals. Its first decades were dominated by civil wars among the conquerors and by the resistance of the Neo-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inca-empire">Inca</a> State in Vilcabamba, whose last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tupac-amaru">Túpac Amaru</a>, was captured and executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cusco">Cuzco</a> in 1572 on the orders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#toledo">Toledo</a>. On the southern frontier the Spanish in Chile fought a long war against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mapuche">Mapuche</a>, financed by an annual subsidy, the <em>real situado</em>, sent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a>. The Pacific coast suffered raids by English and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#dutch">Dutch</a> interlopers: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francis-drake">Francis Drake</a> seized ships at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#callao">Callao</a> in 1579, and a Dutch fleet under Jacques l'Hermite blockaded the port in 1624. In response the viceroys walled Callao in the 1640s and Lima in the 1680s, and after the earthquake and tsunami of 1746 destroyed Callao, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#real-felipe-fortress">Real Felipe</a> fortress was built there. From the 1760s Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manuel-de-amat-y-junyent">Manuel de Amat y Junyent</a> expanded the colonial militias. During the wars of independence Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jose-fernando-de-abascal">José Fernando de Abascal</a> turned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a> into the royalist base of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a>, sending armies that crushed revolutionaries in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quito">Quito</a> and reconquered Chile after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-rancagua">Battle of Rancagua</a> in 1814.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-the-indies.jpg" alt="Council of the Indies" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Royal_Emblem_of_the_Council_of_the_Indies.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown Author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">viceroy</a>, appointed by the king on the advice of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#council-of-the-indies">Council of the Indies</a>, was the Crown's personal representative: he presided over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#real-audiencia-of-lima">Audiencia of Lima</a>, commanded the armed forces, supervised the treasury and exercised royal patronage over the Church. Further <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#audiencia">audiencias</a> were established at Panama, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bogota">Santa Fe de Bogotá</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-peru">Charcas</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quito">Quito</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#santiago">Santiago de Chile</a>, each with judicial and some governing powers. Provinces were run by <em>corregidores</em>, who collected tribute and organised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mita">mita</a> but became notorious for forcing goods on indigenous communities through the <em>reparto</em>. Viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-alvarez-de-toledo">Francisco de Toledo</a> (1569–1581) gave the system its lasting shape: he carried out a general inspection of the highlands, resettled scattered indigenous villages into planned towns, the <em>reducciones</em>, fixed tribute assessments and issued ordinances on mine labour that remained in force for two centuries. Royal treasury offices, the <em>cajas reales</em>, kept accounts across the territory, and viceroys faced a <em>residencia</em>, a judicial review of their conduct, at the end of their terms. In the seventeenth century the sale of offices allowed American-born <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#criollo-people">creoles</a> to buy seats on the audiencia and treasury posts, weakening the Crown's control. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bourbon-reforms">Bourbon reforms</a> reversed this trend: the intendancy system introduced in 1784 replaced the corregidores with salaried intendants, and a new audiencia was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cusco">Cuzco</a> in 1787.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#royal-commentaries-of-the-incas"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/royal-commentaries-of-the-incas.jpg" alt="Royal Commentaries of the Incas" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#royal-commentaries-of-the-incas">Royal Commentaries of the Incas</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Garcilaso_de_la_Vega_Commentarios_Reales_1609.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vega, Garcilaso de la, 1539-1616, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">Silver</a> from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> reshaped the early modern world economy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a> and the other Andean mines produced a large share of the bullion that financed the wars of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#habsburg-dynasty">Spanish Habsburgs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, contributed to rising prices across the continent and, carried across the Pacific to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#manila">Manila</a>, paid for Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#porcelain">porcelain</a>, binding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>, Europe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#asia">Asia</a> into a single trading system. The silver peso, struck at Potosí and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a>, circulated as an international currency. Lima, the City of the Kings, became the political and cultural capital of Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-university-of-san-marcos">University of San Marcos</a>, founded in 1551, is among the oldest universities in the Americas, and the first book printed in South America, a catechism in Spanish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#quechua">Quechua</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#aymara">Aymara</a>, appeared there in 1584 following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#third-council-of-lima">Third Council of Lima</a>. Colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a> also produced classic accounts of the Andean past, the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#royal-commentaries-of-the-incas">Royal Commentaries of the Incas</a></em> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inca-garcilaso-de-la-vega">Inca Garcilaso de la Vega</a>, published in 1609, and the illustrated chronicle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#felipe-guaman-poma-de-ayala">Felipe Guaman Poma de Ayala</a>, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cusco-school">Cuzco School</a> of painting blended European and Andean styles. The viceroyalty's administrative divisions shaped the later borders of Peru, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bolivia">Bolivia</a>, Ecuador and Chile, and its legacy of a Lima-centred state and the marginalisation of the indigenous highlands persisted long after independence.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ayacucho"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-ayacucho.jpg" alt="Battle of Ayacucho" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Ayacucho_by_Mart%C3%ADn_Tovar_y_Tovar_(1827_-_1902).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Martín Tovar y Tovar, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> kings of the eighteenth century reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peru">Peru</a>'s size and status in order to govern and defend their empire more effectively. The Viceroyalty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#new-granada">New Granada</a> was carved out of its northern territories in 1717, suppressed in 1723 and permanently restored in 1739, and in 1776 the creation of the Viceroyalty of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#upper-peru">Upper Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> now flowed out through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>. The free trade regulation of 1778 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lima">Lima</a>'s privileged position in Pacific commerce. Heavier taxation and the abuses of the reparto fed the great rebellion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tupac-amaru-ii">Túpac Amaru II</a>, a kuraka who claimed descent from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inca-empire">Inca</a> rulers, in 1780; it spread across the southern Andes and Upper Peru before he was captured and executed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cusco">Cuzco</a> in 1781, and tens of thousands died in the fighting. Peru remained loyal during the early wars of independence, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jose-de-san-martin">José de San Martín</a> landed on the coast in 1820, entered Lima and proclaimed independence on 28 July 1821. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroy-of-india">Viceroy</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jose-de-la-serna">José de la Serna</a> withdrew to the highlands, where the royalists held out until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s forces won at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#junin-region">Junín</a> in August 1824 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#antonio-jose-de-sucre">Antonio José de Sucre</a> defeated and captured La Serna at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-ayacucho">Battle of Ayacucho</a> on 9 December 1824. The last royalist garrison, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#callao">Callao</a>, surrendered in January 1826.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#viceroyalty-of-peru">Viceroyalty of Peru</a> was the centre of Spanish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a> for almost three centuries, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#silver">silver</a> that its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mita">mita</a> labourers dug at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#potosi">Potosí</a> financed the Spanish monarchy and linked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#europe">Europe</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#china">China</a> in the first truly global trading system.</em></p>

